WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:08.720
In The King and many other films, archers&nbsp;
loose arrow after arrow, as if they have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:16.080
never-ending supply. But was that really the case?&nbsp;
Didn’t they run out sooner or later? In reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:22.080
each archer carried only a limited number of&nbsp;
arrows, and a quiver could be emptied quickly. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:27.360
what happened once they were out? How did they&nbsp;
get more arrows? In this video, we’ll explore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:32.560
how English longbowmen were resupplied during the&nbsp;
Hundred Years’ War—and how they ensured they never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:45.680
ran out of ammunition in the middle of battle.
How England Kept Its Archers Supplied with Arrows&nbsp;

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:51.360
The Battle of Ardres in 1351 showed how&nbsp;
disastrous it could be to run out of arrows.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:56.720
John de Beauchamp, the English military governor&nbsp;
of Calais, set out on a raiding expedition with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:03.360
300 men-at-arms and 300 mounted archers when he&nbsp;
was confronted by a much larger French force. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:08.240
men initially fell back toward St. Omer, but&nbsp;
then entrenched themselves in a river bend to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:14.560
make a stand. The French dismounted and charged&nbsp;
the English position three times. Beauchamp’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:21.040
men repelled them again and again—until the&nbsp;
archers ran out of arrows. Forced into close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:26.400
combat with only swords and daggers, the&nbsp;
lightly armed archers stood little chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:36.000
against the third French assault on their flanks.&nbsp;
The result was a crushing defeat for the English.&nbsp;

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:41.760
Of course, longbowmen brought arrows with them&nbsp;
into battle. Arrows were carried in sheaves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:48.960
or bundles, of 24, usually tucked into a belt at&nbsp;
the side or on the back. Each archer carried at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:54.640
least one bundle, and more were issued before&nbsp;
a battle, with most men going into battle with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.640 --> 00:02:02.240
two or three sheaves—48 to 72 arrows in&nbsp;
total. This was a solid starting supply,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:08.560
but far from enough for a prolonged battle,&nbsp;
let alone an entire campaign. A skilled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:14.320
archer could exhaust that stock in minutes.
The exact maximum rate of fire of longbowmen is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:20.000
still debated, but contemporary sources suggest&nbsp;
that anyone shooting fewer than 10 arrows per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:26.400
minute was considered unfit for service. That&nbsp;
figure can therefore be seen as a minimum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:30.800
while the best archers likely shot&nbsp;
much faster. Of course, in practice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:36.080
archers seldom released arrows at their maximum&nbsp;
rate for several minutes at a stretch. Yet in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:40.560
the opening storm of arrows that typically&nbsp;
launched an English battle, consumption was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:45.760
enormous. And when fighting dragged on,&nbsp;
the supply each archer carried was simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:52.320
not enough—as the Battle of Ardres demonstrated.
For major campaigns, the real challenge was not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:57.360
just resupplying archers but ensuring that&nbsp;
enough arrows were produced to begin with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:02.400
Robert Hardy, a leading expert on the English&nbsp;
longbow, estimates that at the Battle of Crécy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:07.120
the English archers loosed more than half&nbsp;
a million arrows, not counting the weeks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:13.360
of campaigning beforehand. Manufacturing such&nbsp;
vast quantities, not to mention transporting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:18.960
them to the battlefield, was a Herculean task&nbsp;
in an age without industrial production. How&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:25.200
medieval England managed to meet this demand&nbsp;
is best shown by the example of Henry V.&nbsp;

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:31.760
Henry V was an ambitious ruler. According to&nbsp;
historian Juliet Barker, he had a clear goal:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:38.080
to secure the French throne. Soon after his&nbsp;
coronation in 1413, the young king began&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:44.800
planning for an expedition to France—the famous&nbsp;
Agincourt campaign. To avoid being drawn into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:51.360
war of attrition against the numerically superior&nbsp;
French, Henry aimed to strike fast and hard. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:56.720
in many English campaigns of the Hundred Years’&nbsp;
War, longbowmen were key to achieving this goal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:03.120
Henry knew they would need hundreds of thousands&nbsp;
of arrows to succeed. His grandfather Edward III’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:09.280
Crécy campaign had impressively demonstrated the&nbsp;
immense strain such operations placed on supply,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:15.360
and the defeat at Ardres had demonstrated just&nbsp;
how disastrous a shortage of arrows could be.&nbsp;

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:22.720
Drawing on this awareness, Henry—widely regarded&nbsp;
as an exceptionally capable administrator—began&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:29.600
preparations as early as 1413, two years before&nbsp;
the start of the campaign. Immediately after his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:35.440
coronation, he banned the sale of weapons and&nbsp;
arrows to the Scots and other foreign enemies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:41.280
A month later, in June 1413, he created a&nbsp;
new royal office and appointed the fletcher,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:48.320
or arrow maker, Nicolas Mynot as “Keeper of the&nbsp;
King’s Arrows.” Stationed in the royal arsenal at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:54.960
the Tower of London, Mynot was given a substantial&nbsp;
budget and a dedicated staff. His responsibility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.960 --> 00:05:00.800
was to oversee the production and purchase of&nbsp;
arrows and to increase stockpiles in the arsenal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:05.520
Mynot quickly launched large-scale production&nbsp;
and placed orders with arrow makers across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:11.840
the country. In August 1413, for example, he&nbsp;
ordered 12,000 arrows from the London fletcher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:18.480
Stephen Seler—only one of many such orders.
Although Henry began his preparations early,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:23.120
he could never have secured enough arrows without&nbsp;
the well-organized production and supply chains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:28.320
already in place. Over the previous decades, the&nbsp;
longbow had become so central to English armies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:34.160
that a remarkable system of mass production&nbsp;
had developed for both weapons and ammunition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:38.480
This proto-industrial network is impressive&nbsp;
not only for the sheer number of arrows it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:44.080
produced but also because arrows themselves&nbsp;
were complex objects made up of many parts.&nbsp;

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:49.360
That complexity only really hits you when you&nbsp;
try to make one yourself. Most of us wouldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:55.520
even know where to start—and in a situation&nbsp;
where civilization suddenly took a nosedive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.520 --> 00:06:01.120
that could be a real problem. Which brings us&nbsp;
to today’s sponsor: The Book: The Ultimate Guide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:06.880
to Rebuilding Civilization. This isn’t your&nbsp;
average survival manual. It’s a beautifully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:11.680
illustrated guide packed with practical&nbsp;
