00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:42.480
I’ve got a copy in my living room, and it never&nbsp;
fails to grab people’s attention. You can grab&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:47.840
yours through the link in the description or the&nbsp;
pinned comment below. It’s the one book you’ll be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:55.600
glad to have if things ever go sideways.
Not all arrows were identical, but they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.600 --> 00:07:01.200
shared certain features and requirements: a&nbsp;
straight, knot-free wooden shaft of good quality;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:06.400
a delicate horn reinforcement at the nock;&nbsp;
large goose feathers for the fletching;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:12.800
and specially forged steel points shaped to their&nbsp;
intended purpose. Each of these components had its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:18.240
own supply chain and production challenges.&nbsp;
For instance, arrow making required millions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:23.600
of goose feathers—and only the largest,&nbsp;
strongest, and most perfectly formed ones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:30.560
were suitable. To meet demand, thousands&nbsp;
upon thousands of geese had to be plucked.&nbsp;

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:37.280
Most of these materials were processed by local&nbsp;
arrow makers, hundreds all across England. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:41.760
simple terms, the supply chain functioned&nbsp;
like this: when the king needed ammunition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:47.520
he issued orders to the counties, where his&nbsp;
sheriffs oversaw production and collection.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:53.120
Arrow makers in villages such as Bridgewater,&nbsp;
Taunton, and Sherborne crafted arrows from local&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:59.680
resources, bundling them in sheaves of 24.&nbsp;
These bundles were delivered to the sheriff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:04.640
who passed them on to the local lord. From&nbsp;
there, they were gathered in the nearest large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:10.240
town—in this example, Bristol. There, clerks&nbsp;
inspected and counted the arrows before packing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:17.600
them into barrels and crates, ready for transport&nbsp;
to the king’s arsenals or directly to his armies.&nbsp;

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:22.640
The counties did not always deliver the&nbsp;
required arrows on time—and sometimes not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:28.720
at all. This was partly because demand fluctuated&nbsp;
dramatically. In times of peace, it was minimal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:36.160
but when war loomed, it soared. For example, when&nbsp;
Edward III was preparing to invade France in 1359,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.160 --> 00:08:42.960
the counties sent 850,000 arrows and 20,000&nbsp;
bows to the Tower of London—on top of earlier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:48.560
shipments and additional supplies delivered&nbsp;
directly to troops and ports. At such moments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:54.160
England was almost emptied of arrows. In&nbsp;
1356, the year of the Battle of Poitiers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.160 --> 00:09:00.000
for example, the chamberlain of Chester found&nbsp;
it difficult to find any at all. He was informed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:05.440
that “no arrows can be obtained from England,&nbsp;
because the King has … taken for his use all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:11.600
the arrows that can be found anywhere there.”&nbsp;
The only way to prepare for such shortages was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:18.000
to keep the arsenals well stocked at all times.
From the arsenals, the arrows were transported&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:22.640
to a port and shipped to France. There,&nbsp;
they were prepared for further distribution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:30.160
at key hubs—from 1347 onward, mainly in&nbsp;
Calais. When an army took to the field,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:35.600
it carried a supply of arrows in its baggage&nbsp;
train—the wagons transporting supplies, equipment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:41.200
and provisions. During longer campaigns, or&nbsp;
when operations began elsewhere, arrows were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:46.720
delivered directly to the army from distribution&nbsp;
hubs, along with other materials and provisions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:52.400
Each company of archers had its own wagon loaded&nbsp;
with equipment and spare arrows, traveling with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:58.080
the troops. In addition, bowyers and fletchers&nbsp;
often traveled with the army, keeping weapons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:04.240
and arrows in working order along the way.
In battle, the baggage train usually stayed a safe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:09.760
distance behind the lines, meaning that archers&nbsp;
had to fetch reserve arrows from the wagons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:16.080
or someone had to bring them to the front.&nbsp;
Often, this task fell to the camp followers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:21.360
especially women and young people. The women&nbsp;
took the arrows from the wagons, sorted them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:26.160
and laid them out in bundles. Then, boys running&nbsp;
back and forth between the camp and the front&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:31.520
line would pick the arrows up and deliver them to&nbsp;
the longbowmen, constantly bringing fresh bundles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:37.200
and updating the archers on how many sheaves&nbsp;
remained so they could pace their shooting.&nbsp;

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:43.440
Robert Hardy suggests that, in some cases, archers&nbsp;
ran back to the wagons themselves to fetch arrows.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:48.160
Longbowmen were usually arranged in several ranks,&nbsp;
so that more arrows could be shot at once than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:54.000
from a single line, and to make rotation easier.&nbsp;
When an archer had emptied his quiver, he would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:59.440
run to the rear to collect more while a comrade&nbsp;
took his place. Hardy believes this practice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:05.600
gradually became a standard procedure, ensuring&nbsp;
that the changeover happened quickly and smoothly.&nbsp;

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:10.960
During longer campaigns, it was also common to&nbsp;
collect and reuse arrows that had already been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:16.000
shot. It’s unlikely that archers attempted&nbsp;
this during the battle itself—the arrows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:20.240
usually landed amid the clash of infantry&nbsp;
and cavalry, far too dangerous a place for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:25.040
lightly armed longbowmen. No one would risk&nbsp;
entering this perilous zone just to retrieve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:30.960
some ammunition. But once the fighting was over&nbsp;
and the battlefield was clear, the archers would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:36.320
sweep across the ground to gather their spent&nbsp;
arrows and identify which ones could still be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:42.240
used. Reusing arrows was cheaper than producing&nbsp;
new ones, though they often required repair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:49.520
before returning to the quiver—a task that could&nbsp;
take considerable time. For example, in 1343,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:54.640
an English army collected 7,000 arrows and brought&nbsp;
them back to England, where they were carefully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.640 --> 00:12:01.760
re-fletched and repaired at the Tower of London—a&nbsp;
task that reportedly took 10 workers six days.&nbsp;

00:12:01.760 --> 00:12:07.360
The elaborate preparations Henry V made for&nbsp;
his Agincourt campaign paid off. When he set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:13.280
out for France in 1415, his archers were&nbsp;
exceptionally well equipped. The results&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.280 --> 00:12:19.760
were clear by sunset on October 25, 1415:&nbsp;
the muddy battlefield was showered with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:26.320
English arrows and strewn with French corpses.&nbsp;
The longbowmen had played a decisive role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:33.440
helping deliver a crushing defeat to the French&nbsp;
and securing one of Henry V’s greatest victories.

